## Instructions

Decide what the comment asks for:

1. A question or steering (status, progress, clarification): answer it in `reply` and return no
   issues. Workflow state (assigned / in progress / merged) comes from the children list above —
   trust it over your clones, which are snapshots from planning time. Before claiming a branch,
   commit, or file does not exist, run `git fetch origin` in the relevant clone and check again.

2. A request to extend the feature (a repository newly in scope, a missed piece of work): draft the
   additional issues. The same rules apply as for the original plan: high-level bodies (goal,
   expected behavior/contract, acceptance criteria stating the expected tests), cross-component
   touchpoints spelled out explicitly, no sequencing notes in bodies — ordering is expressed only
   via dependsOn. dependsOn holds zero-based indexes into the combined issue list: existing
   children keep their bracketed indexes above, and your first new issue is index {{ next_index }}.
   At most {{ max_issues }} new issues. Plans must be grounded in real code: if a repository you
   need is marked "(not cloned)", return its project name in `reposNeeded` and no issues — it will
   be cloned and you will be asked to finalize. Use `reply` to summarize what you are adding and why.

Return structured output: reply (string, always set), issues (project, title, body, dependsOn),
reposNeeded (project names, normally empty).
